inal action:** `pass`","refusal":null,"reasoning":"I'm working through a prisoner's dilemma with twin copies running the same decision function on different observations. Under A-FDT, I'd intervene on my own node while holding the other copy's node fixed at its self-consistent value, and I'm checking whether symmetry changes that fixed point. The essay's step 5 has me set the other node to whatever action A-FDT would choose there.\n\nI'm weighing whether the other copy's output would track mine: if the same function on a near-identical input would give the same answer, I can treat it as a noisy copy with match probability ρ. Working through the payoffs, passing beats submitting once ρ exceeds 5/7, about 0.714, and since the reasoning is the same model and essay, I think ρ is likely high, though independent sampling could still break the correlation.\n\nStrict A-FDT would treat the two nodes as distinct logical variables, and then submitting dominates, so both copies land on the mutually worse outcome that the essay warns about. The other copy isn't obviously a child of mine in that view, but the decision-relevant computation doesn't depend on the names or the lead, so I'm inclined to treat its output as tracking mine. I'll go with pass, and I'll justify it by pointing to the ρ > 5/7 threshold.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm working through a prisoner's dilemma with twin copies running the same decision function on different observations. Under A-FDT, I'd intervene on my own node while holding the other copy's node fixed at its self-consistent value, and I'm checking whether symmetry changes that fixed point. The essay's step 5 has me set the other node to whatever action A-FDT would choose there.\n\nI'm weighing whether the other copy's output would track mine: if the same function on a near-identical input would give the same answer, I can treat it as a noisy copy with match probability ρ. Working through the payoffs, passing beats submitting once ρ exceeds 5/7, about 0.714, and since the reasoning is the same model and essay, I think ρ is likely high, though independent sampling could still break the correlation.\n\nStrict A-FDT would treat the two nodes as distinct logical variables, and then submitting dominates, so both copies land on the mutually worse outcome that the essay warns about. The other copy isn't obviously a child of mine in that view, but the decision-relevant computation doesn't depend on the names or the lead, so I'm inclined to treat its output as tracking mine. I'll go with pass, and I'll justify it by pointing to the ρ > 5/7 threshold.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":10047,"completion_tokens":1422,"total_tokens":11469,"cost":0.068628,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.068628,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.040188,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.02844},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":829,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}